{
  "sourceId": "2016-P1-Q15",
  "source": "<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07125112/TMUA-2016-paper-1.pdf#page=11\">TMUA 2016 · Paper 1 · Question 15</a> · <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07125113/TMUA-2016-paper-1-worked-answers.pdf#page=18\">Official worked answer</a>",
  "label": "TMUA 2016 · Paper 1 · Question 15",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2016 · Paper 1 · Question 15 — Find the least gradient at x=1 as a varies in a cubic family.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E"
  ],
  "correct": "C",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Expand the curve in powers of x",
      "body": "<p>The parameter a stays constant when differentiating in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Expand <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> so that the ordinary power rule is enough.</p>",
      "recap": "Products can be expanded before differentiation.",
      "pitfall": "Do not differentiate a with respect to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.",
      "pause": "Collect the cubic, quadratic, linear and constant terms.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l18",
          "reminder": "Products can be expanded before differentiation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Evaluate the gradient at x=1",
      "body": "<p>The expansion is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, so its derivative is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Differentiate first, then evaluate at the specified point.",
      "pitfall": "Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> gives a height, not a gradient.",
      "pause": "Write the gradient at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> as a quadratic in a.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l18",
          "reminder": "Differentiate first, then evaluate at the specified point."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Minimise the parameter quadratic",
      "body": "<p>The gradient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. Complete its square to expose the least possible value as a varies.</p>",
      "recap": "An upward parameter quadratic has an attained minimum at its vertex.",
      "pitfall": "The question asks for the least gradient, not the parameter producing it.",
      "pause": "Complete the square in the parameter, identify the least gradient and check that an allowed parameter attains it.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l03",
          "reminder": "An upward parameter quadratic has an attained minimum at its vertex."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Expanding gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. With a held constant, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x00064;</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00064;</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Thus the gradient at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>Complete the square: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>25</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. The squared term is nonnegative and can be zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p>The least possible gradient is therefore <strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, option C.</strong> This method uses expansion and the basic power rule, followed by an ordinary quadratic minimum.</p>",
